WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.340
The neurons at the back of your cerebral cortex are as old as you are.

2
00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:08.840
How could anyone know that?

3
00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:11.480
A cell carries no birth certificate.

4
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:14.880
Part of the answer came from nuclear bomb tests.

5
00:00:15.733 --> 00:00:19.553
Carbon -14 is made at a nearly steady rate in the upper atmosphere.

6
00:00:20.033 --> 00:00:22.653
Then came above -ground nuclear weapons tests.

7
00:00:23.333 --> 00:00:29.353
At their height, between 1955 and 1963, they nearly doubled the level in northern

8
00:00:29.353 --> 00:00:35.553
hemisphere air. After the treaty of 1963 banned tests in the atmosphere, the level

9
00:00:35.553 --> 00:00:36.733
fell year by year.

10
00:00:40.217 --> 00:00:42.137
Plants fix that carbon dioxide.

11
00:00:42.777 --> 00:00:45.397
People eat the plants, or animals that ate them.

12
00:00:45.897 --> 00:00:49.137
The carbon ends up in the DNA of every cell that divides.

13
00:00:49.557 --> 00:00:53.197
So each new cell records the carbon -14 level of that year.

14
00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:55.190
Now think about a neuron.

15
00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:59.990
A neuron that has stopped dividing normally does not copy its DNA again.

16
00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:04.030
So its DNA keeps the carbon -14 of its birth year.

17
00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:09.190
Researchers sorted nuclei from post -mortem brain into neurons and other cells.

18
00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:12.750
Neurons of the occipital cortex were as old as the person.

19
00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:14.410
The other cells were younger.

20
00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:17.910
A sample holds many nuclei, so the value is an average.

21
00:01:18.783 --> 00:01:19.803
What about the hippocampus?

22
00:01:20.703 --> 00:01:26.183
One carbon -14 estimate, from 2013, was that about one third of neurons there are

23
00:01:26.183 --> 00:01:27.363
subject to exchange.

24
00:01:27.843 --> 00:01:30.523
That is about 700 new neurons per day.

25
00:01:31.023 --> 00:01:33.963
Then two groups looked for young neurons in human tissue.

26
00:01:34.683 --> 00:01:40.183
In 2018, one group reported that none were detected in adults aged 18 to 77.

27
00:01:40.803 --> 00:01:45.743
The next month, another reported thousands in healthy people aged 14 to 79.

28
00:01:45.743 --> 00:01:48.083
The question is still argued over.

29
00:01:48.867 --> 00:01:51.027
Why would careful laboratories disagree?

30
00:01:51.687 --> 00:01:53.307
The arguments are about method.

31
00:01:53.867 --> 00:01:58.667
The stain for young neurons weakens with time after death, so a missing signal may

32
00:01:58.667 --> 00:01:59.907
mean the marker was lost.

33
00:02:00.687 --> 00:02:04.427
It is also debated whether the markers label only newborn neurons.

34
00:02:05.127 --> 00:02:09.587
The carbon -14 estimate rests on assumptions, including a turnover model.

35
00:02:10.227 --> 00:02:15.087
Newer studies lean toward some adult neurogenesis, though its rate and role remain

36
00:02:15.087 --> 00:02:19.207
uncertain. So ask what a study can show, and what it cannot.
